<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macario Sakay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macario Sakay was a Filipino revolutionary and member of the Katipunan who refused to accept American rule after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> declared the Philippine-American War over in 1902. He revived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> Republic, or Republic of Katagalugan, and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance in the mountains of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. Induced to surrender in 1906 by promises of amnesty, he was tried as a bandit and hanged in 1907.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macario-sakay.jpg" alt="Macario Sakay" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macario_Sakay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
